  "executive_briefing": "The corporate tax landscape in mid-2026 is defined by a fundamental divergence between global multilateralism and national protectionism. The United States has formally withdrawn from OECD Pillar One and Pillar Two agreements, choosing instead to focus on the 'One Big Beautiful Bill Act' which recalibrates GILTI and FDII deductions to incentivize domestic holdings. This unilateralism extends to trade, where the US administration faces significant legal hurdles after the Court for International Trade (CIT) ruled certain IEEPA-based tariffs unlawful, even as effective tariff rates fluctuate between 15-18%. In contrast, approximately 140 jurisdictions continue to integrate Pillar Two's 15% minimum tax, creating a high-friction environment for MNEs navigating conflicting regimes. Compliance is becoming hyper-digitalized, evidenced by Singapore's InvoiceNow mandate and a global shift toward Continuous Transaction Controls (CTCs), effectively ending the era of post-audit reporting in favor of real-time transactional validation.",
  "trends": [
